Helios DXVK fork: descriptor-buffer set writes reuse the bindings of clean stages (opt-in)

Conduit guest/windows. Applied after 0022 by ci/windows/Build-Driver.ps1.

On NVK DXVK binds resources through descriptor buffers. Merged graphics
pipelines have two sets, one for the views and one for the uniform buffers
of all stages. When anything in a set is dirty, the whole set is written
into new descriptor memory: for every binding the CS thread looks up the
resource, its descriptor or buffer range, checks it for graphics stores and
tracks it in the command list. Re-binding one constant buffer of the vertex
shader re-reads and re-tracks every constant buffer of the pixel shader
too, and one VS shader resource view does the same for every PS view. Each
binding costs a few dependent loads into view, image and buffer objects on
the CS thread.

The dirty mask is kept per stage and class (buffer, view). With
HELIOS_DXVK_DESCRIPTOR_REUSE=1 a set write keeps, per layout type and set,
what each binding held (descriptor pointer or uniform buffer range). A
binding whose stage and class are not dirty takes that value without the
lookup and the tracking; the new set memory is still written whole by the
descriptor worker, so nothing reads descriptor memory the GPU may use.
Stage-local bind changes gain (XXSetConstantBuffers(1), XXSetShaderResources
of one stage); a Map(WRITE_DISCARD) of a constant buffer dirties the buffers
of every stage it may be bound to (invalidateBuffer), so it gains nothing.

The stored values are used only:
- for the same pipeline bindings and set layout, in the same submission
  (tracking ID): a new command list, a render pass begin and a pipeline
  change dirty all graphics stages, so the first write after them is a full
  write and refreshes every entry;
- on the graphics path without full hazard tracking (not compute, not
  unsynchronized render passes);
- for read-only bindings whose resource had no graphics stores when it was
  written, no storage images or buffers, no implicit resolves and no shared
  images (the Helios sampled-shared hook still sees each of those per write).

A clean binding still holds the same view or buffer slice, so its descriptor
(owned by the resource allocation's view map) and its range are what a
full write would produce. Bindings of sets that are not dirty at all are
never re-checked by DXVK either; this applies the same rule per stage.

Default off: no measurement on NVK yet. The first context logs one line when
it is on. The legacy descriptor-set path and the descriptor-heap path are
unchanged.

